  "reference_text": "Most people try to build better habits by relying on motivation, and that is the first mistake. Motivation is a feeling, and feelings change from one day to the next. A system, on the other hand, keeps working even when you do not feel like it. Here is a simple example from my own week. I wanted to read more, so I put one book on my desk where I make coffee every morning. That single change removed the decision, and reading became automatic within four days. Why does this work so well? Because you are designing the environment instead of fighting your own willpower. The second tip is to make the first step almost embarrassingly small. Instead of promising to exercise for an hour, promise to put on your shoes. Once the shoes are on, the next step is much easier. This is not a trick, it is basic psychology, and the research on habit formation supports it. The third mistake is trying to change five things at once. Pick one behaviour, track it for two weeks, and only then add another. If you do this consistently, you will notice that the habit stops feeling like a task.",
  "hypothesis_text": "Most people try to build better habits by relying on motivation, and that is the first mistake. Motivation is a feeling, and feelings change from one day to the next. A system, on the other hand, keeps working even when you do not feel like it. Here is a simple example from my own week. I wanted to read more, so I put one book on my desk where I make coffee every morning. That single change removed the decision, and reading became automatic within four days. Why does this work so well? Because you are designing the environment instead of fighting your own willpower. The second tip is to make the first step almost embarrassingly small. Instead of promising to exercise for an hour, promise to put on your shoes. Once the shoes are on, the next step is much easier. This is not a trick, it is basic psychology, and the research on habit formation supports it. The third mistake is trying to change five things at once. Take one behavior, track it for two weeks, and only then add another. If you do this consistently, you will notice that the habit stops feeling like a task.",
